  "limitations": [
    "Study focused only on relationships between natural environment and components of attitudes, lacking objective behavioral measures (e.g., duration of stay, number of visits, nature stimuli count).",
    "Results need validation in other service environments (retail stores, health facilities, libraries, education institutions) and different countries to observe cultural differences.",
    "Influence of different moderators (gender, age, companions) was not analyzed.",
    "Results must be cautiously generalized due to being a first approach to structural relationships, specific city location, and specific age distribution of the study sample."
  ],
  "minimum_safe_summary": "This empirical study investigated the effects of natural environments in shopping malls on visitors' cognitive image, emotional response, and behavioral intentions using a survey of 292 mall visitors and structural equation modeling. Findings indicate that a natural environment positively influences both cognitive image and affective responses. Cognitive image, in turn, positively affects affective responses and behavioral intentions. Affective responses were found to be a stronger predictor of behavioral intentions than cognitive image. The study also revealed that natural environments have a significant indirect effect on behavioral intentions, mediated by both cognitive image and affective responses, with affective responses playing a more prominent mediating role. Limitations include the lack of objective behavioral measures, the need for cross-cultural validation, and the absence of moderator analysis (e.g., gender, age).",
